---
import Page from '../components/Page.astro';
import { RUBRIC_VERSION } from '../lib/rubric';
import { CONTENT_LAST_UPDATED } from '../lib/build-metadata';

const toc = [
  { id: 'mission', n: 'Mission' },
  { id: 'maintainer', n: 'Maintainer' },
  { id: 'independence', n: 'Independence' },
  { id: 'disputes', n: 'Corrections & disputes' },
  { id: 'licenses', n: 'Code & data licenses' },
  { id: 'contact', n: 'Contact' },
];
---

<Page
  pageTitle="About"
  description="Who maintains defirisk.co, why it exists, and how to challenge a grade."
  crumbs={[{ n: 'About' }]}
  toc={toc}
  hero={{
    h1: 'About',
    lede: 'Who maintains defirisk.co, why it exists, and how to challenge a grade.',
    meta: [
      { label: 'Last updated', value: CONTENT_LAST_UPDATED.about },
      { label: 'Maintainer', value: '0x_Abdul' },
      { label: 'Independence', value: 'No paid grades' },
    ],
  }}
>
  <h2 id="mission">Mission <a class="alink" href="#mission">#</a></h2>
  <p>
    defirisk.co is a structural risk assessment of DeFi protocols. We grade what we can
    verify against an open rubric, and we are candid about what we cannot. The dashboard does not
    predict exploits; it exposes structural risk gaps in protocol deployments. An assigned grade
    is a protocol's standing against that rubric, nothing more.
  </p>

  <h2 id="maintainer">Maintainer <a class="alink" href="#maintainer">#</a></h2>
  <p>
    defirisk.co is maintained by <a class="link" href="https://x.com/0x_abdul">0x_Abdul</a>.
    The maintainer holds authority on the rubric and the public repo.
  </p>

  <h2 id="independence">Independence <a class="alink" href="#independence">#</a></h2>
  <p>
    Independence is the only thing defirisk.co has to sell. There are no paid grades.
    Protocols cannot pay to be assessed, cannot pay to expedite an assessment, and cannot pay to
    influence one. defirisk.co is an open-source risk transparency dashboard for defi protocols.
  </p>

  <h2 id="disputes">Corrections &amp; disputes <a class="alink" href="#disputes">#</a></h2>
  <p>
    Everything we publish is open to challenge through three channels, depending on what you are
    claiming is wrong:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Factual Correction</strong>: a specific data point is wrong and you can cite a
      source. We re-evidence the factor and the letter recomputes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Grade Dispute</strong>: the facts are right but the rubric was applied incorrectly.
      Resolved through the <a class="link" href="/methodology/#appeals">appeals process</a> by
      re-adjudicating the disputed factor.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rubric Proposal</strong>: the rule itself is miscalibrated. Filed as a public issue,
      reviewed by the maintainer, and either accepted into a future rubric version, deferred, or
      declined with a public note.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every challenge is public from the moment it is filed, and we do not pause grades or redact
    pages while one is open. The full process, including what each channel is for, what counts as a
    verifiable source, and the timelines, lives on the
    <a class="link" href="/contributions/#corrections">Contributions</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2 id="licenses">Code &amp; data licenses <a class="alink" href="#licenses">#</a></h2>
  <p>
    Everything defirisk.co publishes is licensed for unrestricted reuse, subject to
    attribution. We choose permissive licenses deliberately, because a closed-licence risk
    assessment is mostly a marketing surface.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Data</strong>: all grades, evidence factors, citation lists, and derived datasets
      are licensed under <code>CC BY 4.0</code>. Attribution to "defirisk.co, rubric {RUBRIC_VERSION}"
      is sufficient.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Code</strong>: the rubric engine, factor parser, adapter SDK, and site itself are
      licensed under <code>MIT</code>. Source for everything lives at the canonical repo.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rubric definitions</strong>: the <code>rubric/</code> directory (factor
      predicates, category definitions, critical-factor list) is dual-licensed under
      <code>CC BY 4.0</code> and <code>MIT</code>, with attribution to the version tag.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="contact">Contact <a class="alink" href="#contact">#</a></h2>
  <p>
    To correct a fact or dispute a grade, use the
    <a class="link" href="/contributions/#corrections">Contributions</a> channels.
  </p>
  <p>
    For coverage requests for new protocols, new chains, and new sectors, submit a public post on X
    tagging <a class="link" href="https://x.com/0x_abdul">@0x_Abdul</a> with the protocol name,
    chain, and official handle. Requests submitted publicly are logged and prioritised against the
    published criteria.
  </p>
  <p>For any other inquiries, DM the X account.</p>
</Page>
